well i this this is the the news that  none of us wanted to  report or indeed to hear but you'll  remember in the first wave of  covert 19 part of the explanation for  why the uk had such a high death toll  was that  many care homes faced a high exposure to  covert 19 and you eventually had about  27 000  excess deaths in care homes getting on  for half of the total death toll  during this pandemic was in care homes  and the hope was that if there was going  to be a second wave then  at the very least we would be able to  protect care homes you wouldn't see a  big spike  in cases in care homes but i have got  hold of  some internal nhs data for  at least one pocket of the country which  shows that the story is  well worryingly familiar if anything  worse than in the spring it's for leads  and  these nhs data show and this space this  line shows you the number of cases  active cases within care homes in the  leeds area  and what you can see is it rose very  sharply in the first  wave of the pandemic around april may  june  and got up to about 150 160 or so  active cases in care homes and leads and  then came down  but just look at what's happened over  the course of the last it is literally  the last week and a bit  you've seen them rise incredibly sharply  from around 20 cases  up to over 200 so that now the number of  care home  cases of covert 19 that we have in this  area of the country and leeds  is actually higher than it was in the  spring so not only is there evidence of  spread to care homes and leads  like in many parts of the country we saw  a rise in cases of young people  of covert 19 but it's now looked like it  has spread in the community and has  spread into care homes but also just the  absolute level  is very high indeed a few provisos it  may well be that the level is  particularly high because we're doing  more testing there is more testing  happening in care homes  and also it's worth saying what we don't  know from these statistics is how many  of these cases  are residents versus the carers the  employees that work there and  i'm told that a chunk of them uh  potentially the majority are people who  work there but we don't have that  breakdown what we do have is this and  there's unpublished  nhs data and it is very alarming indeed  because as i say the hope was  that care homes would be protected  protected in the second wave the hope  was that you wouldn't see  covert spread from the community into  these care homes where there are many  elderly many vulnerable people  but unfortunately now we know for the  first time that in at least one part of  the uk  that spread is happening and the rise of  cases is very worrying indeed
 

